#v(0.3em)

// ========= 1.1 — scalar sigmoid, forward + numerical backward =========
*1.1 A scalar sigmoid unit.* Consider the scalar model
$f = sigma(W x + b)$.

*(a)* Decompose the forward pass into its primitive operations.
#h(1fr) (2 pts)

*(b)* Using the variables from (a), draw the computation graph.
#h(1fr) (2 pts)

*(c)* For $x = 1$, $W = 2$, $b = 3$, target $y = 1$, and squared-error
loss $L = (f - y)^2$, compute the forward pass and the gradient at each
node of the graph. You may annotate the graph or write forward and
backward values for each operation.
#h(1fr) (6 pts)

*(d)* Derive $partial f \/ partial W$ analytically. You may use
$sigma'(z) = sigma(z)(1 - sigma(z))$.
#h(1fr) (1 pt)

#ans[
  *(a)*
  $ z_1 &= W x             && quad "(multiply)" \
    z_2 &= z_1 + b          && quad "(add)" \
    f   &= sigma(z_2)       && quad "(sigmoid)" $

  *(b)*
  #align(center, canvas(length: 1cm, {
    import draw: *
    circle((0, 1.2), radius: 0.28, name: "W"); content("W", $W$)
    circle((0, 0),   radius: 0.28, name: "x"); content("x", $x$)
    rect((1.2, 0.3), (2.0, 0.9), name: "mul"); content("mul.center", $times$)
    rect((2.8, 0.3), (3.6, 0.9), name: "add"); content("add.center", $+$)
    circle((3.2, 2.0), radius: 0.28, name: "b"); content("b", $b$)
    rect((4.4, 0.3), (5.2, 0.9), name: "sig"); content("sig.center", $sigma$)
    content((6.1, 0.6), $f$, name: "f")
    line("W", "mul", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("x", "mul", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("mul", "add", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("b", "add", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("add", "sig", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("sig", (6.0, 0.6), mark: (end: ">"))
  }))

  *(c)* _Forward._
  $ z_1 = W x = 2, quad z_2 = z_1 + b = 5, quad f = sigma(5) approx 0.9933, quad
    L approx 4.48 times 10^(-5). $

  _Backward._
  $ (partial L)/(partial f)   &= 2(f - y) approx -1.34 times 10^(-2) \
    (partial L)/(partial z_2) &= 2(f-y) f(1-f) approx -8.90 times 10^(-5) \
    (partial L)/(partial z_1) &= (partial L)/(partial b) = (partial L)/(partial z_2) approx -8.90 times 10^(-5) \
    (partial L)/(partial W)   &= (partial L)/(partial z_1) dot x approx -8.90 times 10^(-5), quad
    (partial L)/(partial x)   = (partial L)/(partial z_1) dot W approx -1.78 times 10^(-4) $

  *(d)* $ (partial f)/(partial W) = sigma'(W x + b) dot x
    = sigma(W x + b)(1 - sigma(W x + b)) dot x. $
  At $x = 1$, $b = 0$ this is $sigma(W)(1 - sigma(W))$.
]

// ========= 1.2 — sigmoid derivative curve, chain rule, dead gradient =========
*1.2 The sigmoid-derivative curve.* @fig-sigprime plots the
$partial f \/ partial W$ formula you derived in 1.1(d), evaluated at
$x = 1$, $b = 0$, as a function of $W$.

#figure(
  image("plot_1_4.pdf", width: 55%),
  caption: [],
) <fig-sigprime>

*(a)* Explain the shape of the curve in @fig-sigprime.
#h(1fr) (1 pt)

*(b)* Using the chain rule, derive $partial L \/ partial W$ for the
scalar model under $L = (f - y)^2$.
#h(1fr) (1 pt)

*(c)* Explain why $partial L \/ partial W = 0$ when $f = y$.
#h(1fr) (1 pt)

*(d)* State the gradient descent update rule for $W$. Why would large
initial values of $W$ lead to poor loss minimization?
#h(1fr) (2 pts)

#ans[
  *(a)* Bell, symmetric about $W = 0$, peak $1/4$ at $W = 0$; decays to
  $0$ in the tails as $sigma$ saturates and one of $sigma$, $(1 - sigma)$
  vanishes.

  *(b)* $ (partial L)/(partial W) = 2(f - y) dot sigma'(W x + b) dot x. $

  *(c)* $f - y = 0 ⇒ partial L \/ partial W = 0$. A perfect fit leaves
  nothing to minimize.

  *(d)* $W arrow.l W - eta (partial L)/(partial W)$. Large $|W|$ pushes
  $z$ deep into the sigmoid tails where $sigma'(z) approx 0$, so the
  update vanishes — the "dead gradient" regime, in which $W$ barely
  moves per step.
]

// ========= 1.3 — two-layer scalar =========
*1.3 Two-layer scalar composition.* Now consider the two-layer
composition
$ G = sigma( W_2 sigma(W_1 x + b_1) + b_2 ) $
and the same squared-error loss $L = (G - y)^2$.

*(a)* Draw the computation graph for the full model and loss.
#h(1fr) (1 pt)

*(b)* Compute the forward pass and the gradient at each node, for
scalar values $x = 1$, $W_1 = 2$, $b_1 = 1$, $W_2 = 4$, $b_2 = 1$,
target $y = 1$.
#h(1fr) (4 pts)

*(c)* Explain why the gradient of $W_1$ is much smaller than the
gradient of $W_2$.
#h(1fr) (1 pt)

#ans[
  *(a)*
  #v(0.3em)
  #align(center, canvas(length: 0.85cm, {
    import draw: *
    circle((0, 1.2), radius: 0.26, name: "W1"); content("W1", $W_1$)
    circle((0, 0),   radius: 0.26, name: "x");  content("x", $x$)
    rect((1.0, 0.3), (1.7, 0.9), name: "m1"); content("m1.center", $times$)
    rect((2.4, 0.3), (3.1, 0.9), name: "a1"); content("a1.center", $+$)
    circle((2.75, 1.9), radius: 0.26, name: "b1"); content("b1", $b_1$)
    rect((3.8, 0.3), (4.5, 0.9), name: "s1"); content("s1.center", $sigma$)
    rect((5.4, 0.3), (6.1, 0.9), name: "m2"); content("m2.center", $times$)
    circle((5.75, 1.9), radius: 0.26, name: "W2"); content("W2", $W_2$)
    rect((6.8, 0.3), (7.5, 0.9), name: "a2"); content("a2.center", $+$)
    circle((7.15, 1.9), radius: 0.26, name: "b2"); content("b2", $b_2$)
    rect((8.2, 0.3), (8.9, 0.9), name: "s2"); content("s2.center", $sigma$)
    rect((9.8, 0.3), (10.5, 0.9), name: "sub"); content("sub.center", $-$)
    circle((10.15, 1.9), radius: 0.26, name: "y"); content("y", $y$)
    rect((11.3, 0.3), (12.2, 0.9), name: "sq"); content("sq.center", $(dot)^2$)
    content((13.0, 0.6), $L$)
    content((9.35, 1.0), text(size: 7pt)[$G$])

    line("W1", "m1", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("x",  "m1", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("m1", "a1", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("b1", "a1", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("a1", "s1", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("s1", "m2", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("W2", "m2", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("m2", "a2", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("b2", "a2", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("a2", "s2", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("s2", "sub", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("y", "sub", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("sub", "sq", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("sq", (12.9, 0.6), mark: (end: ">"))
  }))

  *(b)* With $h = sigma(W_1 x + b_1)$, $z_2 = W_2 h + b_2$:

  _Forward._
  $ z_1 = 3, quad h approx 0.9526, quad z_2 approx 4.810, quad
    G approx 0.9919, quad L approx 6.53 times 10^(-5). $

  _Backward._
  $ (partial L)/(partial G)   &= 2(G - y) approx -1.62 times 10^(-2) \
    (partial L)/(partial z_2) &= 2(G-y) G(1-G) approx -1.30 times 10^(-4) \
    (partial L)/(partial W_2) &= (partial L)/(partial z_2) dot h approx -1.23 times 10^(-4),
      quad (partial L)/(partial b_2) approx -1.30 times 10^(-4) \
    (partial L)/(partial h)   &= (partial L)/(partial z_2) dot W_2 approx -5.18 times 10^(-4) \
    (partial L)/(partial z_1) &= (partial L)/(partial h) dot h(1-h) approx -2.34 times 10^(-5) \
    (partial L)/(partial W_1) &= (partial L)/(partial z_1) dot x approx -2.34 times 10^(-5),
      quad (partial L)/(partial b_1) approx -2.34 times 10^(-5) $

  *(c)* The chain to $W_1$ passes through one more sigmoid than the
  chain to $W_2$, picking up the first-sigmoid derivative
  $h(1-h) approx 0.045$ together with an upstream $W_2 = 4$ factor; the
  $h$ at the tail of the $W_2$ chain cancels in the ratio, giving
  $partial L \/ partial W_1 approx W_2 (1 - h) dot partial L \/ partial W_2
  approx 0.19 dot partial L \/ partial W_2$ — the $tilde.op 5 times$ gap.
  With more layers, this saturation factor compounds.
]

#pagebreak(weak: true)

// ========= 1.4 — fully-connected + init regimes =========
*1.4 Fully-connected variant and weight initialization.* Consider a
fully-connected variant of the two-layer model from 1.3:
$ h = sigma(bold(W)_1 x + b_1), quad G = sigma(bold(W)_2 h + b_2), $
where the input $x in RR^2$ is now a 2-feature vector,
$bold(W)_1 in RR^(4 times 2)$ and $bold(W)_2 in RR^(1 times 4)$ are
weight matrices (boldface to distinguish them from the scalar $W_1$,
$W_2$ of 1.3), $b_1 in RR^4$, $b_2 in RR$, and $h in RR^4$ is the
hidden activation. The scalar chain from 1.3 corresponds to a single
_stream_ of this network — one entry of $bold(W)_1$ times one entry of
$bold(W)_2$, shown as the highlighted path in @fig-fcn — which reduces
exactly to the scalar 1.3 model only when every off-path entry of
$bold(W)_1$ and $bold(W)_2$ is zero.

#v(0.3em)

#figure(
  canvas(length: 1.0cm, {
  import draw: *
  let dim = (stroke: rgb(0, 0, 0, 60) + 0.6pt)
  let hot = (stroke: rgb("#e6781e") + 1.6pt)

  circle((0, 2.0), radius: 0.32, name: "x1",
         stroke: rgb("#e6781e") + 1.6pt); content("x1", $x_1$)
  circle((0, 0.4), radius: 0.32, name: "x2"); content("x2", $x_2$)
  circle((2.5, 2.4), radius: 0.32, name: "h1",
         stroke: rgb("#e6781e") + 1.6pt); content("h1", $h_1$)
  circle((2.5, 1.6), radius: 0.32, name: "h2"); content("h2", $h_2$)
  circle((2.5, 0.8), radius: 0.32, name: "h3"); content("h3", $h_3$)
  circle((2.5, 0.0), radius: 0.32, name: "h4"); content("h4", $h_4$)
  circle((5.0, 1.2), radius: 0.32, name: "G",
         stroke: rgb("#e6781e") + 1.6pt); content("G", $G$)

  for a in ("x1","x2") {
    for b in ("h1","h2","h3","h4") {
      line(a, b, ..dim, mark: (end: ">"))
    }
  }
  for b in ("h1","h2","h3","h4") {
    line(b, "G", ..dim, mark: (end: ">"))
  }

  line("x1", "h1", ..hot, mark: (end: ">"))
  line("h1", "G",  ..hot, mark: (end: ">"))
  content((1.25, 2.45), text(fill: rgb("#e6781e"), size: 8pt)[$bold(W)_1$])
  content((3.75, 2.05), text(fill: rgb("#e6781e"), size: 8pt)[$bold(W)_2$])
}),
  caption: [],
) <fig-fcn>

We train this network on the two-moons binary classification dataset
(@fig-moons). The four loss curves (A–D) in @fig-inits correspond to
four different weight initializations ($eta = 0.01$ for $5000$ epochs):
A — all zeros;
B — Gaussian ($W_(i j) tilde cal(N)(0, 1)$);
C — large magnitude ($W_(i j) tilde cal(N)(0, 10^2)$);
D — symmetric ($W_(i j) = 1$ for every entry of $bold(W)_1$ and $bold(W)_2$).

#figure(
  image("plot_moons.pdf", width: 42%),
  caption: [],
) <fig-moons>

#figure(
  image("plot_1_5.pdf", width: 100%),
  caption: [],
) <fig-inits>

*(a)* Decompose the forward pass into its constituent operations,
annotating the shape of each intermediate at every step.
#h(1fr) (2 pts)

*(b)* In one sentence per plot, explain the behavior of each of the
four curves, tying back to your analyses in 1.2 and 1.3.
#h(1fr) (4 pts)

*(c)* The dataset is a binary classification task. In addition to the
squared-error loss we have used so far, name one other loss that would
be appropriate here and briefly justify the choice.
#h(1fr) (1 pt)

#ans[
  *(a)*
  $ z_1 &= bold(W)_1 x + b_1 && in RR^4 \
    h   &= sigma(z_1)        && in RR^4 \
    z_2 &= bold(W)_2 h + b_2 && in RR \
    G   &= sigma(z_2)        && in RR $

  *(b)*
  - _A — zero._ Flat at $0.25$: all weights $0$, so first-layer gradients
    carry a $W_2 = 0$ factor (1.2b) and every hidden unit is identical;
    only $b_2$ learns, giving constant $G approx 0.5$.
  - _B — Gaussian._ Healthy. $|z|$ near $0$ keeps $sigma'(z)$ near its
    max $1/4$ (1.2a); the 1.3 stream propagates gradient cleanly; units
    break symmetry.
  - _C — large._ Stuck. $|z|$ enormous so $sigma'(z) approx 0$ (1.2a);
    the stream in 1.3 multiplies two such vanishing factors; only $b_2$
    drifts.
  - _D — symmetric ($W = 1$)._ Descends, then plateaus above B.
    Gradients flow, but every hidden unit sees identical
    $z_j = x_1 + x_2$ and the same gradient — the four units update in
    lockstep, so the net has the capacity of one unit.

  *(c)* _Binary cross-entropy:_
  $L_("BCE") = -[y log f + (1-y) log(1-f)]$. Two reasons: (i) MLE for
  Bernoulli targets, penalizing confident wrong predictions more
  steeply; (ii) with sigmoid output,
  $partial L_("BCE") \/ partial z = f - y$ — the output $sigma'$
  factor cancels, removing the saturation slowdown MSE suffers.
]

#pagebreak(weak: true)

// ========= 1.5 — LayerNorm =========
*1.5 LayerNorm.* Consider an extension of the model in 1.4 where a
_LayerNorm_ is inserted after the first linear layer and before the
hidden sigmoid:
$ z_1 = bold(W)_1 x + b_1, quad hat(z)_1 = (z_1 - mu) / s, quad h =
  sigma(hat(z)_1), $
where $h in RR^4$ is the hidden activation passed to the output layer
(same role as in 1.4), and $mu$, $s$ are the per-sample mean and
standard deviation of $z_1$ across the four hidden units:
$ mu = 1/4 sum_(i=1)^4 (z_1)_i, quad
  s = sqrt( 1/4 sum_(i=1)^4 ((z_1)_i - mu)^2 ). $

@fig-ln shows training loss on the moons data under the same poor
(large-magnitude) initialization as 1.4(C), both with and without LN
(left panel). The right panel shows the magnitude of the gradient at
every entry of $bold(W)_1$ and $bold(W)_2$, captured at epoch $1000$
(dashed grey line in the left panel), when the performance of the
non-LN model is only slowly improving.

#figure(
  image("plot_ln.pdf", width: 90%),
  caption: [],
) <fig-ln>

*(a)* Redraw the computation graph for a single stream of this
modified network.
#h(1fr) (2 pts)

*(b)* What is the added term to the chain of gradients flowing through
the network?
#h(1fr) (1 pt)

*(c)* Using the $partial f \/ partial W$ curve in @fig-sigprime,
explain how LayerNorm shifts the pre-activation $z_1$ toward the
region where $sigma'$ is large, and why this makes the LN variant
more _robust_ to different initialization scales of $bold(W)_1$.
#h(1fr) (3 pts)

*(d)* Using the right panel of @fig-ln, explain why the LayerNorm
variant converges much faster than the no-LN model.
#h(1fr) (1 pt)

*(e)* Using the $partial f \/ partial W$ curve in @fig-sigprime,
explain why the gradient magnitude is larger at both $bold(W)_1$ and
$bold(W)_2$ under LayerNorm.
#h(1fr) (1 pt)

#ans[
  *(a)* Single stream, with LN inserted between the first add and the
  first sigmoid:
  #v(0.3em)
  #align(center, canvas(length: 0.85cm, {
    import draw: *
    // layer 1
    circle((0, 1.2), radius: 0.26, name: "W1"); content("W1", $W_1$)
    circle((0, 0),   radius: 0.26, name: "x");  content("x", $x$)
    rect((1.0, 0.3), (1.7, 0.9), name: "m1"); content("m1.center", $times$)
    rect((2.4, 0.3), (3.1, 0.9), name: "a1"); content("a1.center", $+$)
    circle((2.75, 1.9), radius: 0.26, name: "b1"); content("b1", $b_1$)
    rect((3.8, 0.3), (4.8, 0.9), name: "ln"); content("ln.center", text(size: 8pt)[LN])
    rect((5.5, 0.3), (6.2, 0.9), name: "s1"); content("s1.center", $sigma$)
    // layer 2
    rect((7.1, 0.3), (7.8, 0.9), name: "m2"); content("m2.center", $times$)
    circle((7.45, 1.9), radius: 0.26, name: "W2"); content("W2", $W_2$)
    rect((8.5, 0.3), (9.2, 0.9), name: "a2"); content("a2.center", $+$)
    circle((8.85, 1.9), radius: 0.26, name: "b2"); content("b2", $b_2$)
    rect((9.9, 0.3), (10.6, 0.9), name: "s2"); content("s2.center", $sigma$)
    content((11.3, 0.6), $G$)
    // h label on bridge arrow
    content((6.65, 1.0), text(size: 7pt)[$h$])

    line("W1", "m1", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("x",  "m1", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("m1", "a1", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("b1", "a1", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("a1", "ln", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("ln", "s1", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("s1", "m2", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("W2", "m2", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("m2", "a2", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("b2", "a2", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("a2", "s2", mark: (end: ">"))
    line("s2", (11.2, 0.6), mark: (end: ">"))
  }))

  *(b)* For a single stream, treating $mu$ and $s$ as fixed scalars,
  $hat(z)_1 = (z_1 - mu) \/ s$ gives
  $ (partial hat(z)_1)/(partial z_1) = 1/s. $
  The chain therefore picks up an extra factor of $1/s$ — LN rescales
  backward gradients by the reciprocal of the pre-activation standard
  deviation. (The full Jacobian across all four units additionally
  couples them through $mu$ and $s$, but the single-stream factor is
  simply $1/s$.)

  *(c)* @fig-sigprime shows $sigma'$ peaked at $1/4$ when the argument
  is $0$ and decaying to $0$ in the tails. LN pulls $z_1$ to mean $0$
  and unit variance across the hidden units, so every pre-activation
  sits within $tilde.op plus.minus 2$ of the peak — regardless of
  $|bold(W)_1|$.

  Without LN, $|z_1|$ scales with $|bold(W)_1|$: small init lands near
  the peak and training works; large init lands in the dead tails and
  training stalls — so the trajectory is sensitive to the init scale.
  With LN, $hat(z)_1$ has mean $0$ and unit variance regardless of
  $|bold(W)_1|$, and $sigma'(hat(z)_1)$ is decoupled from the init
  scale. This does not make LN _strictly_ better (a well-tuned init
  matches it, see 1.4B), but it removes init-scale as something the
  practitioner must get right.

  *(d)* Right panel: LN gradients sit $tilde.op 2 times$ above no-LN
  at both layers. Under $W arrow.l W - eta nabla_W L$, a larger
  gradient produces a larger per-step change; over $5000$ epochs LN
  accumulates many more useful updates and the loss descends
  accordingly.

  *(e)* From (c), LN keeps $sigma'(hat(z)_1) approx 1/4$; without LN,
  $sigma'(z_1) approx 0$. The $sigma'$ factor enters the chain
  _directly_ for $bold(W)_1$ and _indirectly_ for $bold(W)_2$ —
  saturated $h$ pushes $z_2$ into saturation too, whereas healthy $h$
  under LN keeps $z_2$ off the tails. Saturation therefore propagates
  to both layers.
]

#pagebreak(weak: true)

// ========= 1.6 — KNN contrast =========
*1.6 K-nearest neighbors.* A _K-nearest-neighbors_ (KNN) classifier
assigns to each input the majority label among its $K$ closest points
in the training set (by Euclidean distance). Unlike the sigmoid MLP
from 1.4, KNN has no trainable parameters — it simply stores the
training data and computes distances at prediction time.

@fig-boundary shows the decision boundaries of four classifiers on the
moons training data: the MLP from 1.4(B) (Gaussian init, trained to
convergence) and three KNN classifiers. Which is which has been
anonymized as "Model A"–"Model D". The training set contains
$N_0 = 106$ class-$0$ points and $N_1 = 94$ class-$1$ points
($N = N_0 + N_1 = 200$).

#figure(
  image("plot_boundary.pdf", width: 100%),
  caption: [],
) <fig-boundary>

*(a)* Name one practical advantage and one practical disadvantage of
KNN relative to the trained MLP.
#h(1fr) (2 pts)

*(b)* The NumPy skeleton below implements a KNN classifier for binary
labels. Fill in the three blanks (marked `...`) so that the function
returns the predicted label for each row of `X_test`.
#h(1fr) (3 pts)

```python
import numpy as np

def knn_predict(X_train, y_train, X_test, K):
    """
    X_train: (N, d) training inputs
    y_train: (N,)   training labels in {0, 1}
    X_test:  (M, d) query points
    Returns: (M,) predicted labels in {0, 1}
    """
    preds = np.zeros(X_test.shape[0])
    for i, x in enumerate(X_test):
        # 1. Euclidean distances from x to every training point: shape (N,)
        dists = ...
        # 2. indices of the K nearest training points
        idx   = ...
        # 3. majority label among those K neighbors
        preds[i] = ...
    return preds
```

*(c)* What is the computational cost of a single prediction at test
time, as a function of the training-set size $N$ and the input
dimension $d$? How does this compare with a forward pass through the
2-4-1 MLP from 1.4?
#h(1fr) (1 pt)

*(d)* The sigmoid MLP was trained by gradient descent. Why can a KNN
classifier _not_ be trained the same way?
#h(1fr) (1 pt)

*(e)* Three of the four panels (A, B, C) show KNN classifiers with
$K in {1, 5}$ together with the MLP. Match each of Models A, B, C to
one of $\{$MLP, KNN with $K = 1$, KNN with $K = 5\}$, and justify your
assignments from the shape of the boundaries.
#h(1fr) (2 pts)

*(f)* Panel D shows a KNN classifier with $K = N = 200$. Explain why
its prediction is constant across the plane, referring to the class
balance stated above.
#h(1fr) (1 pt)

#ans[
  *(a)* _Advantages._ Zero training cost — just store the data, no
  optimization or hyperparameters beyond $K$; and no parametric
  assumption, so the class of boundaries KNN can express grows with
  the dataset. _Disadvantages._ Prediction is $O(N d)$ per query, so
  inference scales with the training-set size; the classifier is
  sensitive to label noise, where a single mislabelled point near the
  boundary directly flips predictions in its neighbourhood; and
  performance degrades in high dimensions where Euclidean distances
  become uninformative.

  *(b)*
  ```python
  dists    = np.linalg.norm(X_train - x, axis=1)
  idx      = np.argsort(dists)[:K]
  preds[i] = (y_train[idx].mean() > 0.5).astype(float)
  ```
  Line 1 broadcasts $x$ against the $(N, d)$ training matrix and takes
  the row-wise Euclidean norm to get an $(N,)$ distance vector. Line 2
  sorts the distances and keeps the $K$ smallest indices. Line 3
  averages the neighbor labels (in ${0, 1}$ the mean is the fraction
  of class-1 votes) and thresholds at $1\/2$, equivalent to the
  majority rule $hat y = bb(1)[sum_(i in cal(N)_K) y_i > K\/2]$.

  *(c)* $O(N d)$ per query: $O(d)$ to compute each of the $N$
  training-point distances, then $O(N)$ to find the $K$ smallest. The
  2-4-1 MLP is $O(d H + H) = O(d H)$ with $H = 4$ hidden units —
  independent of $N$. KNN prediction therefore scales with
  training-set size; the MLP's does not.

  *(d)* KNN has no trainable parameters — the "model" is the training
  data itself. Any continuous handle enters through an $arg min$
  inside the nearest-neighbor lookup, which is piecewise-constant, so
  the gradient of the loss with respect to it is zero almost
  everywhere. Gradient descent therefore has nothing useful to
  minimize over.

  *(e)* $A = $ KNN $K = 5$; $B = $ MLP; $C = $ KNN $K = 1$.
  - _C (K=1):_ most jagged, each training point owns a small Voronoi
    cell; label noise shows up as tiny outlier islands.
  - _A (K=5):_ piecewise-linear with moderate pockets — local voting
    smooths the $K = 1$ cells but still can't produce a continuous
    curve.
  - _B (MLP):_ single smooth curve — the $0.5$ level set of
    $sigma(bold(W)_2 sigma(bold(W)_1 x + b_1) + b_2)$ is necessarily
    continuous and is sculpted by only four hidden sigmoids.

  *(f)* With $K = N$ every query sees the entire training set, so the
  neighbor average equals the global mean label $N_1 \/ N = 94/200 =
  0.47 < 0.5$ at every point. The majority vote therefore returns
  class $0$ everywhere — KNN at $K = N$ reduces to the constant
  majority-class predictor.
]

